### What is the All Tasks Scheduling Orders problem?

Given a number of tasks and prerequisite pairs, enumerate every valid order in which all tasks can be completed. Unlike the single-order variant, this problem requires listing all possible topological orders. This is a combination of topological sort with backtracking.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;

public class AllTaskOrders {

    public static List<List<Integer>> findAllOrders(int taskCount, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] inDegree = new int[taskCount];
        boolean[] used = new boolean[taskCount];

        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {
            adj.get(pre[0]).add(pre[1]);
            inDegree[pre[1]]++;
        }

        List<List<Integer>> results = new ArrayList<>();
        LinkedList<Integer> partial = new LinkedList<>();

        backtrack(adj, inDegree, used, partial, taskCount, results);
        return results;
    }

    private static void backtrack(List<List<Integer>> adj, int[] inDegree,
            boolean[] used, LinkedList<Integer> partial, int taskCount,
            List<List<Integer>> results) {

        if (partial.size() == taskCount) {
            results.add(new ArrayList<>(partial));
            return;
        }

        for (int t = 0; t < taskCount; t++) {
            if (!used[t] && inDegree[t] == 0) {
                // Choose task t
                used[t] = true;
                partial.add(t);
                for (int next : adj.get(t)) inDegree[next]--;

                // Recurse
                backtrack(adj, inDegree, used, partial, taskCount, results);

                // Undo (backtrack)
                used[t] = false;
                partial.removeLast();
                for (int next : adj.get(t)) inDegree[next]++;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Why does backtracking enumerate all orders?

At each step, every task with in-degree 0 is a valid next choice. By trying each one, recursing, and then undoing the choice, you systematically explore every possible sequence. The undo step restores the in-degree array and the used flags so the next iteration at the same level sees the original state.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

public class BacktrackingDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        // Tasks: 0, 1, 2
        // Prerequisites: 0->2, 1->2
        // In-degrees: [0, 0, 2]

        // Level 0: tasks 0 and 1 both have in-degree 0
        //   Branch 1: choose 0
        //     Level 1: task 1 has in-degree 0
        //       Choose 1 → reduce 2 to 0
        //         Level 2: task 2 has in-degree 0
        //           Choose 2 → order complete: [0, 1, 2]
        //         Undo 2
        //       Undo 1
        //   Undo 0
        //   Branch 2: choose 1
        //     Level 1: task 0 has in-degree 0
        //       Choose 0 → reduce 2 to 0
        //         Level 2: task 2 has in-degree 0
        //           Choose 2 → order complete: [1, 0, 2]
        //         Undo 2
        //       Undo 0
        //   Undo 1
        // Results: [[0,1,2], [1,0,2]]

        System.out.println(AllTaskOrders.findAllOrders(3,
            new int[][]{{0,2},{1,2}}));
    }
}
```

```text
[[0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 2]]
```

### Why is this exponential?

For n independent tasks (no prerequisites), every permutation is a valid topological order, giving n! orders. With dependencies, the count is smaller but can still grow rapidly. This makes the algorithm impractical for large graphs and only useful for small problem sizes.

```java
// Independent tasks:
// 3 tasks, 0 prerequisites → 3! = 6 orders
// 4 tasks, 0 prerequisites → 4! = 24 orders
// 10 tasks, 0 prerequisites → 10! = 3,628,800 orders

// With dependencies:
// 3 tasks, 0→1, 1→2 → only 1 order: [0, 1, 2]
// 4 tasks, 0→2, 1→3 → 4 orders (interleaving two chains)
```

### How does the undo step work?

After the recursion returns, you must restore the state to what it was before the choice. This means setting `used[t] = false`, removing the task from the partial order, and incrementing the in-degree of all dependents back to their pre-choice values.

```java
// Choose:
used[t] = true;
partial.add(t);
for (int next : adj.get(t)) inDegree[next]--;

// Recurse:
backtrack(...);

// Undo:
used[t] = false;
partial.removeLast();
for (int next : adj.get(t)) inDegree[next]++;
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(V! * V) worst case | Factorial orders, V work per order |
| Space | O(V + E) | Adjacency list + recursion stack |
| Practical limit | Small graphs only | 10-15 tasks at most |

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include zero tasks (return one empty order or no orders), fully connected chain (only one valid order), fully disconnected (n! orders), and a cycle (no valid orders).

```java
package com.bank.audit;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        // Chain: 0->1->2 → only [0, 1, 2]
        System.out.println(AllTaskOrders.findAllOrders(3,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{1,2}}));

        // Independent: 0, 1 → [0,1] and [1,0]
        System.out.println(AllTaskOrders.findAllOrders(2, new int[][]{}));

        // Cycle: 0->1, 1->0 → no valid orders
        System.out.println(AllTaskOrders.findAllOrders(2,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{1,0}}));
    }
}
```

```text
[[0, 1, 2]]
[[0, 1], [1, 0]]
[]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the undo step, which corrupts the state for sibling branches. Another mistake is modifying the in-degree array without restoring it, causing later branches to see incorrect prerequisite counts.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: no undo after recursion
    // The used flags and in-degrees stay modified
    // Sibling branches see corrupted state
    // Fix: always undo used[t], partial, and in-degrees after recursion
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, audit reviewers want to see every legal sequence for a small set of dependent audit steps. Different reviewers have different staffing constraints, so they pick the sequence that fits best. The backtracking enumeration gives them all options.

```java
package com.bank.audit;

import java.util.List;

public class AuditOrderPlanner {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 0: Risk Assessment
        // 1: Document Review
        // 2: Field Verification
        // 3: Final Report
        int[][] prerequisites = {{0, 3}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}};
        List<List<Integer>> orders = AllTaskOrders.findAllOrders(4, prerequisites);

        String[] names = {"Risk Assessment", "Document Review",
                          "Field Verification", "Final Report"};
        System.out.println("Total valid orders: " + orders.size());
        for (List<Integer> order : orders) {
            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < order.size(); i++) {
                if (i > 0) sb.append(" -> ");
                sb.append(names[order.get(i)]);
            }
            System.out.println("  " + sb);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Total valid orders: 6
  Risk Assessment -> Document Review -> Field Verification -> Final Report
  Risk Assessment -> Field Verification -> Document Review -> Final Report
  Document Review -> Risk Assessment -> Field Verification -> Final Report
  Document Review -> Field Verification -> Risk Assessment -> Final Report
  Field Verification -> Risk Assessment -> Document Review -> Final Report
  Field Verification -> Document Review -> Risk Assessment -> Final Report
```
